 ## Subtopics
 
-### Standard DDIM Sampling
-Uses `guidance_scale=1.0` (no CFG) for unconditional or conditional generation without the amplification effect [G]. Choose when guidance artifacts are undesirable [G]. [Link](8117-diffusion-based-guidance-for-radiance-field-optimization.md)
-
-### Refinement Mode
-Uses high `guidance_scale` (e.g., 100) and `strength < 1.0` to add detail to existing latents [S1, unofficial]. Choose when enhancing existing structures rather than generating from noise [G]. [Link](8117-diffusion-based-guidance-for-radiance-field-optimization.md)
+### NeRF Interpolation via DDIM and SLERP
+A procedure to generate smooth transitions between 3D samples by interpolating in the diffusion latent space before deterministic decoding [S2]. It samples two initial diffusion latent values $x^{(T)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$, interpolates them using spherical linear interpolation (SLERP), and passes the result through a deterministic solver [S2]. Standard Gaussian diffusion models often produce non-smooth interpolation results, and models trained with long schedules for unconditional generation yield distinct but discontinuous interpolated samples [S2]. Early stopping for sparse-view reconstruction preserves a smoother prior, yielding better generalization and smoother transitions than long unconditional training schedules [S2]. Used in SSDNeRF to interpolate between generated 3D samples [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to `num_inference_steps` and the cost of one UNet forward pass per step [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the latent tensor size and UNet activations [G]. The doubling of latents and embeddings for CFG increases memory usage by roughly 2x during the loop [G].
-- **Performance:** Default settings use 50 steps and a guidance scale of 7.5 for sampling, or 100 for refinement [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Allows fine-grained control over the trade-off between structure preservation and detail addition via `strength` [S1, unofficial]. CFG improves sample quality and adherence to conditions [G].
-- **Limitations:** High guidance scales can lead to oversaturation or artifacts [G]. Refinement requires careful tuning of `strength` to avoid destroying the input structure [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** For generating high-quality images from text prompts or refining latent representations in diffusion-based optimization loops [S1, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and fewer steps are required, or when unconditional generation is sufficient (set `guidance_scale=1`) [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to `num_inference_steps` and UNet inference time per step [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to latent size and UNet memory footprint [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Default 50 steps provide a balance between quality and speed [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** High-quality image generation and refinement; effective guidance via CFG [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Computationally expensive due to iterative denoising; requires careful tuning of `guidance_scale` and `strength` [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to Use:** For high-fidelity image generation or refinement where global structure is important [S1, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use:** For real-time applications or when local consistency is prioritized over global structure (use Inpainting SDS Loss instead) [11185-inpainting-sds-loss.md].
 
 ## Background
-The method is implemented in the DreamGaussian codebase as part of a diffusion-based optimization loop for radiance fields [S1, unofficial]. It provides gradient signals from a frozen Stable Diffusion model by predicting noise, applying classifier-free guidance, and backpropagating the difference between predicted and actual noise [S1, unofficial].
+Originated from the DreamGaussian project, which utilizes DDIM sampling with CFG for 3D-aware image generation and refinement [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on the DDIM sampler from Stable Diffusion pipelines [S1, unofficial]. SSDNeRF extends this concept to 3D NeRF interpolation, highlighting the importance of early stopping for smooth transitions [S2].
 
